{
  "configuration_id": "config_051",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_011 (multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, concat) achieved 0.632 accuracy, a ~10% jump over singleprofile baselines (~0.53), establishing multiprofile as the dominant design choice. The singleprofile image-encoder swap (resnet50→ViT, config_004 vs 031) showed negligible difference, but that was in a weaker regime; in multiprofile, stronger per-profile image representations may matter more when aggregating across profiles. Question: Does upgrading the image encoder from resnet50 to imagenet_vit_l improve accuracy in the multiprofile setting while keeping all other variables (scvi_n200, avgpool, concat) fixed? This isolates the image-encoder contribution. Why this experiment: It is the cleanest single-variable extension of our best result, directly testing the most natural next improvement. If it helps, we have a clear path to push accuracy higher; if not, we rule out the image encoder and pivot to aggregation (transformer) or fusion (weighted) in subsequent experiments. Expected cost: moderately higher than config_011's 7.27 GPU hrs—ViT-L adds per-image overhead across multiple profiles, so I estimate ~8.5–9.5 GPU hrs, which is acceptable given 6 experiments remain and the potential accuracy gain justifies the spend."
}

